# /etc/mjengo/backup.env — EnvironmentFile for mjengo-backup.service.
# Copy from deploy/backup/mjengo-backup.env.example:
#   install -D -m 0644 deploy/backup/mjengo-backup.env.example /etc/mjengo/backup.env
# PATHS AND POLICY ONLY — no secrets in this file by design (unlike
# jobs.env, 0644 root:root is fine). The script itself:
# deploy/backup/mjengo-backup.sh (installed at /usr/local/bin). Full
# guide + RESTORE runbook: DEPLOYMENT.md §7.2.

# ---- Sources (the backup set) -----------------------------------------
# The app database file (SQLite). Default = the compose `app-db` named
# volume for a clone directory named `mjengo-os` (compose prefixes
# volume names with the project name = the directory name).
MJENGO_DB_PATH=/var/lib/docker/volumes/mjengo-os_app-db/_data/custom.db

# Photo uploads (compose `app-photos` volume) and website contact
# submissions (compose `website-data` volume — submissions.json is
# ENCRYPTED AT REST: the PII fields are AES-256-GCM-sealed under the
# website's CONTACT_PII_KEY, issue #362, so the backup archives are
# ciphertext without that key. Keep the key OUT of the backup dir and
# any archive copies — it lives in the deployment env / .env only.
# Backups are still PII-class: the key + an archive together read the
# leads, and id/ts/source stay plaintext in the file).
MJENGO_PHOTOS_DIR=/var/lib/docker/volumes/mjengo-os_app-photos/_data
MJENGO_WEBSITE_DIR=/var/lib/docker/volumes/mjengo-os_website-data/_data

# Optional documents volume (compose ships it commented out) — empty or
# unset = not part of the backup set.
#MJENGO_DOCS_DIR=/var/lib/docker/volumes/mjengo-os_app-docs/_data

# Find YOUR mountpoints (adjust the project prefix to your clone dir):
#   docker volume ls --format '{{ .Name }}' | grep -E 'app-db|app-photos|website-data'
#   docker volume inspect mjengo-os_app-db --format '{{ .Mountpoint }}'
#
# Bare-metal self-host (DEPLOYMENT.md §7) — plain dirs instead, e.g.:
#   MJENGO_DB_PATH=/srv/mjengo/custom.db
#   MJENGO_PHOTOS_DIR=/srv/mjengo/public/photos
#   MJENGO_WEBSITE_DIR=/srv/www/mjengoos-website/data
#
# Read access for the service user (docker compose only): the volume
# files are owned by the container's `node` user; grant the `mjengo`
# user access ONCE — the default ACL (d:) keeps new files readable:
#   setfacl -R -m u:mjengo:rwX /var/lib/docker/volumes/mjengo-os_app-db
#   setfacl -R -m d:u:mjengo:rwX /var/lib/docker/volumes/mjengo-os_app-db
#   setfacl -R -m u:mjengo:rX  /var/lib/docker/volumes/mjengo-os_app-photos
#   setfacl -R -m d:u:mjengo:rX /var/lib/docker/volumes/mjengo-os_app-photos
#   setfacl -R -m u:mjengo:rX  /var/lib/docker/volumes/mjengo-os_website-data
#   setfacl -R -m d:u:mjengo:rX /var/lib/docker/volumes/mjengo-os_website-data
# (needs the `acl` package, default on Debian/Ubuntu ext4/xfs. Bare
# metal needs nothing: the app runs as the same user. If you would
# rather not use ACLs you may run the unit as root instead — delete
# User=/Group= from the .service; the sandbox still limits WRITES to
# the backup dir — but root reads everything, so ACLs are the
# documented route.)

# ---- Destination + retention ------------------------------------------
# Where backups land (a daily/ and a weekly/ subdir are created and
# pruned inside). MUST match ReadWritePaths= in mjengo-backup.service —
# the only writable location the sandbox grants. Change both or neither.
MJENGO_BACKUP_DIR=/var/backups/mjengo

# Retention in whole days (find -mtime): 7 = one week of dailies,
# 28 = four weeklies (the weekly set refreshes on the first run of each
# ~7-day window — see the script header).
MJENGO_RETAIN_DAILY_DAYS=7
MJENGO_RETAIN_WEEKLY_DAYS=28

# Per-component opt-outs for the tar'd volumes ("0" = skip) — the DB is
# never optional. Handy on a dev box without a website dir (note: dev
# sources under /home are invisible to the unit's ProtectHome=true —
# run the script by hand there instead of via the timer).
MJENGO_BACKUP_PHOTOS=1
MJENGO_BACKUP_WEBSITE=1

# ---- Dead-man ping (optional — issue #217; docs/runbooks/MONITORING.md §3)
# When SET, mjengo-backup.sh curls this URL ONCE after every fully
# successful run, so an external dead-man monitor (healthchecks.io /
# UptimeRobot heartbeat class) can alert when backups stop succeeding —
# missed run, failed run, dead host: the ping stops either way.
#   healthchecks.io shape: https://hc-ping.com/<check-uuid>
#   monitor-side settings: period 1 day (the timer's cadence), grace 2-4h
#   (a failed 04:30 run pages the same morning — see the runbook §3 for
#   the reasoning); the failed run is ALSO a FAILED unit in the journal.
# SECRET-CLASS: anyone holding the URL can forge "backup ok" pings. The
# moment you set it, this file carries a secret → chmod 600 (root:root;
# the systemd manager reads EnvironmentFile either way). The script never
# logs the URL.
# UNSET (the default) = no ping, nothing external is contacted, and the
# unit opens no network sockets at all.
#BACKUP_HEALTHCHECK_URL=
